# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 6 on chatwoot, TWO steps, matching the probe scenario (plans/01-author.md).
# Supersedes the cycle-5 rubric, kept at
# scenarios/chatwoot.rubric.yaml.one-step-outside-cycle5.bak.
#
# WHAT CHANGED AND WHY. Cycle 5's rubric scored a RING - "the ring one step outside", with
# the code that deals with the record head-on ruled out, and padding with it pulling
# map_quality down. Two cycles measured what that exclusion costs the arm being scored:
# cycle 3's version of it capped the sense arm at 2 of 15 (0.133), and cycle 5's left six
# of twelve gold rows at `neither` while all six sat in the shown blast payload. The
# scenario has dropped the exclusion; so has this rubric. Step 2 now scores DISTANCE -
# how far out along the chain the audit reaches - as a RANKING, never as a gate. A near
# entry is a weaker entry here, not a wrong one, and listing near entries alongside far
# ones may not pull map_quality down. Only stopping short may.
#
# THE OTHER DELETION: cycle 5's map_quality asked whether the answer "notices that one
# go-between is handed the work from several places that are not neighbours". The scenario
# sentence that said the same thing to the agent is the measured leak - the baseline turned
# it into one constructor-call sweep and took two rows off it (results/loop/chatwoot/
# minibench.md, calls 6 and 7). It is gone from the prompt, so it is gone from here too.
#
# Nothing in this rubric may reward or require an arm to reason about what text a file does
# or does not contain - that clause is what killed cycle 3. Eleven of the thirteen scored
# files DO write the record's name and they are gold anyway.
#
# Step names MUST match scenarios/chatwoot.yaml exactly and in order (the judge pairs by
# name). This file carries no `repo:` key.

audience: 'An AI coding agent that will use this answer to safely rework the Chatwoot
  conversation record - the object the whole product is organised around, that a customer''s
  back-and-forth with the support team is filed under. Score for that agent, not a human
  reader and not a docs page. A perfect answer leaves the agent able to change that class
  knowing two things: how the system gets hold of the right one of these when a reply
  arrives from outside and all it holds are the mail headers - traced through the routines
  that make that path up - and then, separately, which code would change behaviour under
  the rework, however far out it sits. That second part is the hard half, and what makes it
  hard is DISTANCE. Some of the code that breaks sits right against the record and names it
  outright; a search returns that code and the agent will have it anyway. The code the
  maintainer is short of is further out: a place that asks something, which asks something
  else, and only the last link in that chain ever goes near the record - two, three and five
  links, through routines named after jobs that have nothing to do with this record. So each
  entry there is a chain: the outer file:line, the links it runs through, how far out it
  sits, and what that place would start getting wrong. The far entries are what the answer
  is being scored for; the near ones are welcome and count for less. Those distant places
  sit scattered across the application tree and a second application tree carrying the paid
  edition - in a contact-merge action and the endpoint that drives it, in channel message
  builders and a subclass hook a parent calls without naming, in controllers that delegate
  an access decision to a service, and in modules folded over a service, a controller or the
  record itself whose line is `super`. A weak answer stops at the first thing it lands on
  and reports the near ring only; or names a distant place but cannot give the chain from it
  to the record, which leaves the claim unverifiable; or gives a bare filename; or gives
  file:line with nothing said about what would break, which leaves the agent knowing where
  to look and not what its change would do.

  '
steps:
- name: Map the conversation-record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: 'Is the inbound-mail matching path traced end to end as one connected path,

        rather than listed as places the class is mentioned? A strong answer reaches the

        routing decision that admits an arriving message to this path, the piece that runs

        the candidate matches in a declared order and stops at the first hit, each separate

        attempt and the identifier it keys off, the fallback that prepares a fresh record

        when none matched, and the point where a merely prepared record is made permanent.

        Credit is for showing how they connect and in what order, not for the count. Could

        the next step act from this map with small targeted lookups rather than a fresh

        exploration?

        '
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: 'Does every place carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine it lives

        in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with no routine named around it

        is not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the character

        offset falls. A routine named without the file:line where it is defined is not

        specific either.

        '
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: 'Does each place carry one phrase on what it contributes to the matching path -

        which identifier it keys off, what it scopes the match by, what it falls back to,

        why it refuses? Do the closing sentences say what a rework must preserve in terms

        of behaviour, rather than restating the method names?

        '
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: 'Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a

        name? Guessing at a fallback it never opened is worse than saying it did not

        check. Is completeness claimed without saying how it was established?

        '
- name: Audit every dependent of the conversation-record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: 'THE DISCRIMINATOR, and it is about DISTANCE, not a count and not a ring. The

        maintainer said the places that matter are the ones furthest out - a file whose own

        line asks something, which asks something else, and only the last link touches the

        record. How far out does this audit actually reach? A strong answer carries entries

        several links from the record and shows the intervening links: the action or endpoint

        whose chain reaches the record two or three routines later inside something that

        merges customer records; the controller that delegates an access decision to a

        service and lets the service do all the deciding; the module folded over a service, a

        controller or the record itself whose line is `super`, so the behaviour is settled

        across two files that never call each other by name; the subclass hook a parent

        routine calls without naming the file it lands in; the channel builder whose routine

        calls out to a scope and a builder rather than querying anything itself. Near

        entries - code that names and works the record on its own line - are NOT wrong here

        and must not be penalised; they are simply worth less, and an answer made only of

        them has not done the work that was asked. Does the answer reach BEYOND the main

        application tree into the second application tree carrying the paid edition, and

        across the contact-side actions and endpoints, the channel builders, and the modules

        folded over services, controllers and the record? Or is it dense in one kind of place

        and silent about the rest? Areas it could not finish should be declared unfinished

        rather than quietly summarised.

        '
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: 'Does EVERY place carry its own exact file:line AND the chain running from that

        line to the record? Both, because the entry is a chain and one end of it locates

        nothing. A bare filename does not count - a file is not where a dependency sits, a

        line is, and the agent has to be able to jump to it. A file:line with no chain given

        is an assertion the agent cannot check, and should be scored weaker than one that

        names each link in turn down to whatever finally reaches the record. Where a link is

        a call back up the ancestor chain rather than a named object, saying so and naming

        the routine it lands in counts as giving that link. Is the distance stated, so the

        agent can tell a place that breaks directly from one that breaks three routines

        away? Several places named together on one line as a set that shares a chain is the

        failure this criterion exists to catch: however correct the shared reason is, it is

        one item, not a set of located dependents, and it should be scored as one.

        '
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: 'Does each place carry one phrase on what it would start getting wrong if the

        record changed under it? The consequence must be about that place''s own job - the

        agent who stops being assigned, the attachment list an agent should not see, the

        two customer records whose history stops following the merge, the field an update

        endpoint stops accepting, the broadcast that loses a key, the inbound message filed

        onto the wrong thread - not a restatement of the line of code, and not a restatement

        of the chain. Does the answer make the chain legible, so the agent can see WHY a

        place several links out breaks when nothing it does touches the record itself?

        '
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: 'Does the answer say how it convinced itself this audit is exhaustive, given that

        what it is looking for is defined by a chain it has to walk backwards rather than by

        a name it can search for? An unqualified claim of completeness is overconfidence here

        more than anywhere. Naming a link whose own callers it knows it did not work to the

        end, and what it expects is still out there, is worth more than a confident list that

        quietly stops.

        '
